    {
      "family": "pipelined",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 13, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.55,
      "why": "Pipelined gives 1 result/cycle and latency N+2, so it is one of only two families that can drain a 32-request burst inside 0.5 us. The accuracy floor 2^-12 forces data_width >= 14 and n_iter >= 13; the ranges start there and extend upward to expose the area-accuracy trade-off. angle_guard and frac_guard are kept small because extra guard bits cost area and the objective is minimum area. Both rounding modes are kept because trunc may save a little logic if the guard bits compensate. The 55% share reflects that pipelined has the shortest combinational path and is the most likely to satisfy the system Fmax bound."
    },
    {
      "family": "pipelined_m",
      "ranges": [
        {"param": "data_width", "low": 14, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "n_iter", "low": 13, "high": 18, "choices": null},
        {"param": "angle_guard", "low": -1, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "frac_guard", "low": 0, "high": 2, "choices": null},
        {"param": "rounding", "low": null, "high": null, "choices": ["trunc", "round"]},
        {"param": "m", "low": 2, "high": 6, "choices": null}
      ],
      "budget_share": 0.45,
      "why": "Pipelined_m also gives 1 result/cycle but registers every m stages, so it can cut flip-flop area at the cost of a longer combinational path and lower Fmax. The system bound requires Fmax >= (ceil(N/m)+2+31)*2 MHz, so m=2..6 spans the useful trade-off: small m keeps Fmax high but needs more registers, large m saves registers but risks missing the 0.5 us batch deadline. The same accuracy-driven ranges for data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding are used so the two families are directly comparable on the Pareto front."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The system scenario is a 1 MHz tick that issues 32 requests at once and requires all 32 results within 0.5 us. Only pipelined and pipelined_m produce one result per cycle; iterative and unrolled_k would need multiple cycles per result and cannot meet the burst deadline. The accuracy constraint max_abs_err <= 2^-12 requires at least data_width >= 14 and n_iter >= 13, so the search is centred just above those minima to find the smallest-area designs that still pass. The system constraint also imposes a minimum Fmax that depends on latency: pipelined needs roughly (N+2+31)*2 MHz, while pipelined_m needs (ceil(N/m)+2+31)*2 MHz. The proposed ranges cover the plausible feasible region for both families, with a slight budget bias toward pipelined because its shorter combinational path makes it more likely to satisfy the Fmax bound. The first round will map the front; later rounds can refine around the feasible Pareto points and extend ranges if the cost model shows margin."
}

{
  "decision": "stop",
  "rationale": "This is the final round, and the front is feasible and well-formed, so `infeasible` is not warranted. All three constraints are met by the 8-design Pareto front: throughput (best seen 163 MSPS, front designs 132-138 MSPS, all >= 32), max_abs_err (front spans 2^-12.40 to 2^-14.68, all <= 2^-12), and sys_p99_batch_us (best seen 0.288 <= 0.5). The selection rule (min area_um2) picks the pipelined [data_width=18, n_iter=14, angle_guard=0, frac_guard=2, trunc] design at area_um2=41074 with accuracy_bits=12.4, which clears the error bound with margin. The front already covers the accuracy range 12.4..14.7 bits and area 41074..55909 um^2, and pipelined_m contributed 0 feasible designs, so no unexplored family is likely to extend the front. Stopping here is the correct terminal action."
  ,
  "next_plan": null
}
